Abstract
The potentiality of collagen sponge as a skin substitute, derived from mrigal (Cirrhinus cirrhosus) scale has been explored in this study. Acid soluble collagen (ASC) and pepsin soluble collagen (PSC) from the scale of mrigal were isolated and characterized. The yields of ASC and PSC were $\sim3\%$ and $\sim7\%$ based on the dry weight of scale while the hydroxyproline content was $\sim90~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . Scanning electron microscope revealed progressive demineralization with EDTA on time dependent scale. Further, the D-Spacing in fibril bundles were calculated to be $\sim67\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Fourier transform infrared and circular dichroism spectra confirmed extracted protein to be collagen I, where both ASC and PSC comprised of two different $\mathfrak{a}$ -chains ( $\mathbf{\bar{a}}_{1}$ and ${\bf a}_{2}$ ). The denaturation temperature $\mathrm{(T_{d})}$ of the collagen solution was $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ closer to $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of mammalian collagen. In vitro cell culture studies on the extracted collagen sponge showed efficient cell growth and proliferation. Additionally, co-culture with fibroblast and keratinocyte cells showed development of stratified epidermal layer in vitro. Faster wound healing potential of the extracted collagen in a rat model proved its applicability as a dermal substitute.
Keyword: Mrigal Fish Scale, Type I Collagen, RT PCR, wound healing.
## 1. Introduction
Grafting is preferable for the healing of a full-thickness wound of more than one cm in diameter due to lack of dermal tissue [1]. Autografts, allografts, xenografts and cell therapies are the most common therapeutic options to heal full-thickness skin wounds. However, antigenicity, post-operative infections, and limitation of donor sites, restricts the use of the skin substitutes mentioned above [2]. Therefore, tissue engineering approach to promote tissue repair, regeneration, and recovery of their functions is appreciable [3]. Restoring barrier function with accelerated and complete wound regeneration is the cardinal function of the tissue-engineered scaffold. A 3D scaffold provides a platform for cellular migration, adhesion and differentiation leading to the development of new functional tissues. Further, a skin regenerating scaffold potentially prevents wound bed contraction throughout the initial stages of healing [4]. Complementing the 3-D organization of the tissue space, additionally, allows scaffold to cover a maximum surface area, maintaining moisture, and absorbing exudate [5].  A scaffold for skin regeneration should have pore architecture with mean pore size $100{-}200\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and porosity above $90\%$ [6]. Open porous and interconnected networks facilitate cellular migration and proliferation, fast nutritional/waste exchange, resulting in enhanced vascularization and formation of new tissues [7].
Collagen I, being a major extracellular matrix component, plays a pivotal role in maintaining tissue homeostasis, biological integrity and structural mechanics through continuous remodeling [8]. Such prevalent existence and vital activity recommend collagen I as a promising material in skin tissue engineering. During wound healing, matrix metalloproteinases are secreted in the course of a signaling cascade initiated by collagen activated integrins. Metalloproteinases eventually promote keratinocyte migration by degrading provisional collagen matrix; thereby accelerating wound closure [9].  Migrating keratinocytes enhance collagenase production by interacting with collagen scaffold. Additionally, collagen breakdown products are chemotactic for cells required for granulation tissue formation [10, 11]. Collagen based skin substitutes currently available are either decellularized matrices obtained from allogeneic or xenogeneic sources like Integra™ (bovine collagen-GAG scaffold), Alloderm™ (decellularized human dermis), Matriderm™ (bovine lyophilized dermis, coated with $\mathfrak{a}$ -elastin hydrolysate), Biobrane™ (silicone film, nylon fabric, porcine collagen), and Permacol™ (porcine acellular diisocyanite cross-linked dermis) etc [3]. However, associated cattle borne diseases, such as bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE), transmissible spongiform encephalopathy (TSE), foot and mouth diseases (FMD), aesthetic and religious issues/beliefs reduced demand of terrestrial animal origin collagen I. Further, these products have natural or synthetic supporting materials and removal of the non-biodegradable synthetic components need surgical innervations.
In the present manuscript, we report the isolation and characterization of collagen type I from Indian carp mrigal (Cirrhinus cirrhosus). Mrigal, a freshwater fish (FWF), is a species of ray-finned fish belonging to the Cyprinidae family, which is very common and widely distributed in the Southeast Asian countries with an annual catch of more than 380,000 tones [12]. Isolation of collagen I from aquatic animals like fishes, squid, sponge, and jelly fish is well reported [13-16]. Mrigal have cycloid scales with specialized scleroblast cells, which synthesize ECM. This ECM comprising of type I collagen and hydroxyapatite, makes it an attractive collagen source for isolation [17]. Pati et al. isolated collagen from scales of FWF such as Rohu and Catla, and found non-immunogenic in Swiss albino mice [18].  FWF origin collagen has a similar thermal stability to mammalian collagen and comparatively higher than cold water fishes and marine fishes. Further, FWF origin collagen has high imino acid content, which subsequently enhances the crosslinking density, resulting in improved thermal stability. Here, we extracted mrigal scale collagen, characterized, and assessed its purity using various methods. Further, collagen sponges were developed, in vitro and in vivo biocompatibility and wound healing properties were studied in rat models, to evaluate their applicability for the development of novel dermal substitute.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Extraction of collagen
Mrigal scales (C. cirrhosus) were collected, washed with distilled water and air dried prior to collagen extraction. Extraction was carried out at $4~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ via modification of previously described method [19],[13]. Briefly, the scales were dipped into a solution containing NaCl (Merck, India), Tris- HCl (Merck, India), and EDTA (Merck, India) at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ for a period of 48 h to wash off the unwanted proteins from the surface of the scales. An EDTA solution of $0.5{\bf M}$ at $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ was added for demineralization of scales followed by washing with distilled water. Further, the scales were treated with $0.5{\bf M}$ acetic acid solution at $\mathsf{p H}2.5$ for a period of $48\mathrm{~h~}$ and the insoluble part was filtered out. Acid Solubilized Collagen (ASC) was extracted from the filtrate and pepsin-solubilized collagen (PSC) from the residue. The insoluble part was treated with $1\%$ pepsin (Sigma) in $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ acetic acid followed by filtration and the supernatant was dialyzed against 0.02 M ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{HPO}}_{4}$ (Merck, India) in order to inactivate the pepsin. Both the filtrates were precipitated out separately by the addition of NaCl to a final concentration of 0.9 M. The precipitate was re-solubilized in $0.5{\bf M}$ acetic acid. The resulting solution was dialyzed using membrane (molecular weight cut-off range: 12–14 kDa, LA 401, Himedia, Mumbai, India)
against $0.1\textbf{M}$ acetic acid and distilled water consecutively for a period of $24\mathrm{~h~}$ followed by freeze-drying.
## 2.2. Characterization of the extracted collagen
## 2.2.1. Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM)
The fracture surfaces of mineralized scales, scales after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and $48\mathrm{~h~}$ demineralization, were gold coated for 90 sec using a plasma gold sputter, and under an accelerating voltage of 10- $20~\mathrm{KV}$ was observed under SEM (EVO 60, Carl Zeiss SMT, Germany) to unveil the collagen assembly in the upper and lower layer of the scale. Further, SEM microscopic studies were carried out for extracted collagen fibrils (ASC). D spacing was calculated from SEM micrographs using image J software. 20 random fibers were examined for this measurement
## 2.2.2. Hydroxyproline assay
Collagen was analyzed for hydroxyproline content according to the method described by Bergman & Loxley (1963) with slight modification [20]. To the neutralized protein hydrolysate (pH of 6.0–6.5), isopropanol was added in the ratio 1:2, followed by $0.1\ \mathrm{ml}$ of oxidant solution ( $7\%$ (w/v) chloramine T (Merck, India), and acetate/citrate buffer at $\mathrm{pH}\ 6$ in 1:4 ratio (v/v)). Finally, $1.3\mathrm{\ml}$ of Ehrlich’s reagent solution ( $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~2~g~}}$ of $\mathfrak{p}$ -dimethylamino-benzaldehyde (Merck, India), in $3~\mathrm{ml}$ of $60\%$ (v/v) perchloric acid (w/v) and isopropanol at a ratio of 3:13 (v/v)) was added. The mixture was heated at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $25~\mathrm{{min}}$ in a water bath and then cooled in running water. Absorbance was measured against water at $558~\mathrm{nm}$ . A hydroxyproline standard curve was drawn with concentration ranging from 100 to $600\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}$ for analysis.
2.2.3. Sodium dodecyl sulphate–polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE)
Electrophoresis (SDS–PAGE) was performed via modification of Laemmli (1970) method using discontinuous Tris–HCl/glycine buffer system, $7\%$ separating gel, and $4\%$ stacking gel [21]. A collagen solution of $2\mathrm{mg/ml}$ in sample buffer was prepared under both reducing and non-reducing conditions by the addition or exclusion of $2{\cdot}{\upbeta}$ mercaptoethanol (SRL, Mumbai). High molecular weight marker (protein molecular weight marker; MW range 10-315 KDa; Genetix, India) and calf skin type I collagen (Fluka, Sigma Aldrich Chemie Gmbh, Buchs, USA) were run along with samples. Electrophoresis was performed using the mini dual vertical electrophoresis unit (Biorad, India). Band intensity was measured using ImageJ program (NIH Bethesda, MD) for each band and compared.
## 2.2.4. Amino acid analysis
The freeze-dried products were hydrolyzed in an inert atmosphere with 6 N HCl at 105 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $\cdot$ in the presence of $1\%$ phenol (v/v).  The hydrolysates were dried under vacuum, derivatized, and diluted with sample diluents. The prepared samples were analyzed using HPLC (Waters Corporation, Milford, MA). The results were quantitatively analyzed against standard amino acids and reported as the number of residues per thousand amino acid content.
## 2.2.5. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) analysis
Freeze-dried products were individually mixed with KBr and pelleted. Infrared spectra were obtained in the range between 4000 and $1000~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ using an infrared spectrophotometer (Model NEXUS-870, Thermo Nicolet Corporation, Madison, WI, USA).
## 2.2.6. Circular dichroism (CD) measurement
CD spectra were obtained experimentally using J-815 spectrometers (JASCO, Easton, MD) at different temperatures. A collagen solution of $0.05~\mathrm{mg/ml}$ in $0.5{\bf M}$ acetic acid was used and the experiment was done with a path length of $0.2\mathrm{cm}$ at wavelengths $240{-}190\ \mathrm{nm}$ at a scan speed of $50\ \mathrm{nm/min}$ with an interval of $0.5\mathrm{nm}$ . Collagen denaturation temperatures were determined by a rotatory angle at a fixed wavelength of $221\ \mathrm{nm}$ , ([θ] 221, as a function of temperature). The temperature was raised at a rate of $1^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ by controlling with a Peltier system with an experimental error of $\pm0.1^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and the data were collected at every $1~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ temperature rise. The mean molar ellipticity (θ) was calculated using mean residue molecular weight and expressed in deg $\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{dmol}$ . The denaturation temperature, $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ , was determined as the temperature at which the change in ellipticity (θ) was half-complete.
## 2.2.7. Temperature induced viscosity change
The denaturation of collagen in solution was determined from temperature induced viscosity change using an Ubbelohde Viscometer. Collagen solutions of $0.1\%$ (w/v) in $0.1\textbf{M}$ acetic acid were loaded to Viscometer and incubated at $10~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ by submerging in a water bath (JULABO Labortechnik, GmbH, Seelbach, Germany). The temperature was raised stepwise to $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and incubated for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ at each temperature. Fractional viscosity was calculated for each temperature as follows:
Measured viscosity-Minimum viscosity Fractional viscosity $\c=$ Maximun viscosity -Minimum viscosity
Where, the viscosity was specific viscosity $(\upeta_{\mathrm{sp}})$ and was calculated by following the equation:
$$
\eta_{\mathrm{sp}}=\frac{\mathrm{t}-t_{0}}{t_{0}}
$$
Assuming the densities of the solution and solvent were same (t and $\mathbf{t}_{0}$ are the efflux times of collagen solution and solvent, respectively). The denaturation temperature $\mathrm{(T_{d})}$ is the temperature at which fractional viscosity is 0.5, which was estimated with prepared graph.
## 2.3. Preparation of collagen scaffolds
The fish collagen, thus isolated from scale was dissolved in $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ acetic acid. It was dialyzed against $0.1\mathbf{M}$ acetic acid and deionized water to bring the $\mathrm{pH}$ of the collagen solution to ${\sim}6$ which initiates in vitro fibrillogenesis. Dialyzed collagen solution was poured into petri dish and lyophilized. Resulted collagen sponges were cross-linked with $3\%$ glutaraldehyde (v/v) for 2 h, followed by $3\mathrm{~h~}$ glycine treatment to block free aldehyde groups. Subsequently, the samples were washed four times with distilled water and lyophilized [18]. SEM was done for the collagen scaffolds after crosslinking following the procedure mentioned above. Further, scaffold morphology was examined through stereo zoom microscope (Leica, DFC295, Germany).
## 2.3.1. Swelling behavior
Samples $(\sim1~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ were incubated in PBS $\mathrm{(pH}~7.2)$ at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for different time points ranging from 30 minutes to 72 hours. After incubation, samples were wiped gently to remove the excess surface liquid, and sample weights were measured $(\mathrm{W_{f}})$ . Initial dry sample weights were also noted $(\mathrm{W_{i}})$ . Both the measurements were done using microbalance (Mettler Toledo, 162 US) [22]. The percentage swelling of samples $(\mathrm{n}=5)$ were calculated using the following equation
$$
\mathcal{I}_{o}\mathrm{{Swelling}}=[(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{f}}-\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{i}})/\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{i}}]\times100\
$$
## 2.3.2. Biodegradation kinetics
The dry weight of samples $(\mathrm{W_{i}})$ was initially noted, followed by their incubation in $0.1\mathbf{M}$ PBS supplemented with lysozyme $\mathrm{\nabla{10}\ \upmu g/m l)}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(\mathrm{n}=5)$ ). The samples were removed every day, washed thoroughly with distilled water, and air-dried, prior to recording their final weight $(\mathrm{W_{f}})$ . These experiments were carried out for 18 days, after which no sample remnant remained [22]. Percentage weight remaining of samples after degradation was calculated using the following equation:
$$
\%\mathrm{\Weight{\remaining}=(W_{f}/\Delta W_{i})\times100}
$$
## 2.3.3. In vitro cell culture using primary fibroblasts and keratinocyte
Primary fibroblasts and keratinocytes were isolated from human foreskin (0-5 years age) as previously described. Collection and processing of the skin sample were approved and monitored by the institutional ethical committee (IEC), Indian Institute of Technology, Kharagpur. With informed consent of donors, fresh human foreskin was collected during circumcision surgery. Briefly, the foreskins were initially treated with Dispase II (Sigma) overnight at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The epidermis was then stripped off, diced into small pieces and incubated with trypsin-EDTA (Gibco) to loosen the cells. The cells were collected by centrifugation and grown in Keratinocyte specific media (KSFM) with media supplements (Gibco). The dermis was treated with collagenase I (Gibco) at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The dermal cells were collected by centrifugation and suspended in complete Dulbecco’s modified Eagle’s medium (DMEM) with $10\%$ fetal bovine serum (FBS), 100 units/ml penicillin, and $100\mathrm{\mg/ml}$ amphotericin B. After reaching $90\%$ confluency, the cells were passaged at a ratio of 1:3. Fibroblast cells of passages three to eight while keratinocyte cells of passage two to four were used in these studies [23].
## 2.3.3.1. Cell attachment, morphology and viability
Scaffolds were initially sterilized using $70\%$ ethanol for $3\mathrm{~h~}$ , followed by repeated washing with PBS and overnight incubation in cell culture medium. Fibroblast cells were then seeded at a density of $5{\times}10^{4}$ cells/scaffold $(\sim1\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The culture medium was replaced at regular intervals. For evaluating the morphological characteristics of proliferating fibroblast growing on the scaffold, SEM was performed. After 3 and 7 days of culture, the cells were first fixed in $2.5\%$ glutaraldehyde for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ followed by ascending series of aqueous ethanol $(50-100\%)$ ) dehydration and observed under SEM with the conditions detailed in ‘‘Scanning electron microscopy’’ section [22].
Cell metabolic activity was measured using MTT (3-(4,5-Dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2,5- Diphenyltetrazolium Bromide) assay. Cells were seeded at a density of $5{\times}10^{4}$ cells/scaffold, whereas an equivalent number of cells were seeded on tissue culture plate (TCP) as control, and incubated at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .  The culture medium was replaced at regular intervals. At the end of 1, 3, 5 and 7 days, the samples with cell were transferred to fresh wells, washed with PBS and incubated in $0.5~\mathrm{mg/ml}$ MTT solution at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . For TCP, the wells were washed and incubated with MTT solution. The insoluble formazan crystals thus formed were dissolved in DMSO and absorbance was recorded at $595~\mathrm{nm}$ on a microplate reader on Plate screen (RMS, Chandigarh, India) [22].
## 2.3.3.2. Protein adsorption study
Protein adsorption kinetics of the collagen scaffold were measured by incubating the samples in $5\%$ FBS/PBS solution for 1 h, 3 h, 6 h, and $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at $37\ ^{\mathrm{~o}}\mathrm{C}$ , and quantified by bicinchoninic acid (BCA) protein assay. After specified time period samples were taken out, washed thoroughly and incubated with a BCA working solution (50 parts of BCA reagent with one part of $4\%$ copper sulphate pentahydrate, green coloured solution) at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $45\mathrm{min}$ . The amino acids adhered on the scaffold reduce ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions, resulting in a crimson coloured complex with BCA. The coloured complex concentration was quantitatively measured on a UV absorbance spectrophotometer (Model-UV-1601, Shimadzu, Japan) at $562\mathrm{nm}$ [24].
## 2.3.3.3. Fibroblast and keratinocyte co-culture
Fibroblasts were seeded on the scaffold $(1{\times}10^{5})$ and cultured for two days in DMEM media. Thereafter, keratinocytes $(5\times10^{5})$ ) were seeded and grown in KSFM medium for further two days. The scaffold was then placed on a stainless-steel grid and raised to the air–liquid interface and grown for 14 days.  The media during the air liquid interface was a 3:1 mixture of DMEM and Ham’s nutrient mixture F12 medium containing $10\%$ FCS, $5~{\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}}$ insulin, $1\times10^{-10}$ M choleratoxin, $0.4~\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}$ , hydrocortisone, $5~{\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}}$ transferrin, and $1{\times}10^{-11}\mathrm{~M~}$ triiodothyronine [25, 26].
## 2.3.3.4. Reverse transcriptase PCR
Total RNA was isolated from the reconstructed skin equivalent using RNA extraction kit (Himedia, India) according to the manufacturer’s instruction. Reverse transcription was done at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , activation at $65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 min and inactivation at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 min in the thermal cycler (Eppendorf, Germany). The cDNA was amplified by PCR with primers enlisted in table 1. PCR amplification was done for 35 cycles using the following conditions: $94~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (denaturation) for 30 s, Variable 1 (annealing) for $30~\mathrm{s}$ , and $72^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ extension for 30 s and final extension at 72 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 10 min. PCR products were then analyzed by $1\%$ agarose gel electrophoresis.
## 2.3.3.5. TGF-β1 quantification by ELISA
TGF- $\cdot$ cytokine was quantified by enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays (ELISA) according to the manufacturer’s protocol (ThermoFisher Scientific). Culture media of monocultures of fibroblast and keratinocyte on the scaffold and TCP (control) were collected after 3 and 7 days of growth. Further, culture media of co-culture of fibroblast and keratinocyte growing at air-liquid interface were also collected after 3 and 7 days. Protein values were calculated in ng with respect to the standard curve.
## 2.4. Skin wound healing experiment with collagen sponges
The ethical committee of Indian Institute of Technology, Kharagpur, approved the in vivo experiments. Twenty-four adult Wistar rats (Rattus norvegicus) weighing $200\pm50\mathrm{\g}$ were used. The animals were under supervision with respect to general health conditions, and received balanced food and water. They were kept at room temperature with controlled humidity and a 12-h photoperiod.
The rats were initially anesthetized with an intraperitoneal injection of ketamine hydrochloride $(90~\mathrm{mg/kg)}$ and xylazine hydrochloride $\mathrm{(10~mg/kg)}$ , and the dorsal region was shaved. Thereafter, a $2\times2\ c m\ (4\ c m^{2})$ area was outlined with a template, on the dorsal lateral plane, caudally corresponding to the forelimbs [27]. Incisions were made and the square skin flap was removed down to the level of subcutaneous panniculus carnosus. All animals were divided into two groups $\mathrm{{\acute{n}=4}}$ for each group). Group A with full thickness excisional wounds (negative control) and group B with full thickness excisional wound covered with collagen. Sterile transparent TegadermTM dressings were used to cover the wound.  On days 5, 10, and 15, four rats in each group were euthanized, and the reconstituted skin was harvested and then embedded in paraffin for hematoxylin–eosin (HE) and Masson’s trichrome staining. Further,  sections were immunostained  using  a  Biogenex SuperSensitive TM Polymer-HRP  Kit  (Biogenex,  US) according  to  the  manufacturer’s instructions. Primary antibody for P 63 (Abcam, UK) with dilution 1: 200; keratin 10 (Abcam, UK) with dilution 1: 200 and collagen III (Abcam, UK) with dilution 1: 500 were in this study.
Percentage wound closure $\mathbf{\tau}=$ (initial area of wound – ${\{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}}^{\mathrm{th}}}$ day area of wound) / initial area of wound $\times100$
## 2.5. Statistical analysis
All the experiments were carried out in triplicates and means were analyzed using the ttest and expressed as a mean $\pm\mathrm{SD}$ . Differences were considered to be significant at p $<0.05$ .
## 3. Results
## 3.1. SEM of scale fracture surface and isolated ASC
Mrigal scale is composed of type I collagen and hydroxyapatite as extracellular matrices. EDTA treatment for demineralizing the scales resulted in higher fraction of exposed collagen fibers as compared to intact scale as seen in Fig. 1 (A, B and C) [28].These collagen fibers were then extracted in acetic acid solution and further by pepsin treatment.
Within a fibril, tropocollagen molecules are aligned in a staggered parallel manner resulting in repeating gap/ overlap pattern. This gives rise to the characteristic D-spacing, observed as alternate light and dark region. Fig. 1D shows the SEM image of extracted ASC collagen with alternate light and dark banding patterns. The D spacing was calculated to be \~ 67 nm similar to human dermal collagen banding pattern [29] [30].
## 3.2. Collagen yield
In this study, acid solubilized collagen yield was $\sim3\%$ , while pepsin solubilized collagen yield was $\sim7~\%$ . Hydroxyproline content was calculated to be $\sim90~\mathrm{mg/g}$ in scale.   The decrease in the solubility of collagen in acetic acid is likely due to covalent bond crosslinking arising through condensation of aldehyde groups in the telopeptide region along with inter-molecular cross-linking. This intermolecular crosslinking is cleaved during pepsin digestion, while maintaining the integrity of the triple helix, therefore, PSC yield is commonly higher than ASC [31].
## 3.3. SDS-PAGE
Electrophoresis of isolated collagen was carried out; SDS-PAGE patterns of ASC and PSC analyzed under reducing and non-reducing conditions were displayed in Fig. 2A. It was found that both ASC and PSC consisted of two $\mathfrak{a}$ -chains $\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}_{\mathrm{{a}_{1}}}$ and ${\bf{\alpha}}{\bf{{a}}}_{2}$ ) as the major constituents. The molecular weights of ${\bf a}_{1}$ and ${\bf{a}}_{2}$ were approximately 120 KDa and 110 KDa, respectively. Additionally, high molecular weight components, including $\upbeta$ - and $\gamma$ -components, were also observed in both ASC and PSC. The relative staining intensity of ${\bf a}_{1}$ was higher than ${\bf q}_{2}$ , suggesting high amount of ${\bf a}_{1}$ compared to ${\bf{a}}_{2}$ . Furthermore, the relative band intensity of ${\bf a}_{1}$ was approximately twice as compared to ${\bf{a}}_{2}$ as shown in Fig. 2B. Therefore, constitution of the mrigal fish scale collagen could be deduced as $({\bf a}_{1})_{2}{\bf a}_{2}$ [32, 33]. Electrophoretic patterns of ASC and PSC analyzed in the presence and absence of $\upbeta$ -ME were similar, indicating absence of disulfide bonds in ASC and PSC, which is true for collagen I. Although the electrophoretic mobility of ASC and PSC were similar, the band intensity ratio of cross-linked components $\upbeta-$ and $\gamma\cdot$ - components) to total non cross-linked monomer chains ( $\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}_{\mathrm{{a}_{1}}}$ and ${\bf{a}}_{2}$ ) of ASC were visibly higher than that of PSC evident from graph and gel pictures. Hence, ASC had more intra- and intermolecular cross-links than PSC [34]. Conversions of some $\upbeta$ - and $\gamma$ -components in the PSC matrix to $\mathfrak{a}$ -component by the treatment with pepsin lead to the decrease in cross-linked components of PSC. However, it cannot be determined whether ${\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}{\bf{\alpha}}_{{\bf{\alpha}}}$ -chain exists in the collagens, since ${\bf{\alpha}}{\bf{\alpha}}\mathrm{\bf{\alpha}}\mathrm{a}_{3}$ -chain has a migration similar to that of ${\bf a}_{1}$ -chain and it cannot be separated from ${\mathfrak{a}}_{1}.$ - chain under the electrophoretic conditions employed [35]. The extracted collagen was ascertained pure as the collagen molecules consisted primarily of ${\bf a}_{1}$ and ${\bf{a}}_{2}$ chain along with its cross-linked components without admixture of other non-collagenous components.
## 3.4. Amino acid analysis
Amino acid composition is an important characteristic of collagen and serves as an identity. The amino acid composition of ASC is presented in Table 2, and expressed as amino acid residues/1000 total amino acid residues. The scale collagen of mrigal had glycine as the major amino acid and that was nearly $34\%$ of total amino acids. The other high content amino acids found were alanine, glutamic acid, proline and hydroxyproline in descending order, while the contents of tyrosine, histidine were very low, and tryptophan was not detected, like other collagens [13, 19].  The imino acid content (hydroxyproline and proline) of ASC was 200 residues/1000 residues, whilst that of calfskin collagen contained 215 residues/1000 residues. Therefore, mrigal ASC had insignificantly low imino acid content than mammalian collagen, which was in accordance with the earlier reports [33]. The extracted collagen had low methionine and insignificant cysteine in the amino acid composition, which suggests that the collagen from of the mrigal scale is type I collagen [31, 36].
## 3.5. FTIR
The FTIR spectra of isolated ASC, PSC and standard calf skin collagen are shown in Fig. 3A. The amide A band is commonly associated with the $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ stretching vibration and when the NH group of a peptide is involved in a hydrogen bond, the position is shifted to lower frequency [37]. The amide A band was found to be at $3323~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for ASC, $3300{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ for PSC and $3325\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for standard. Amide B band is related to an asymmetrical stretch of $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ and is observed at 2938 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for ASC, $2925~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ for PSC and $2927~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ for standard [38]. The amide I band associated with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching vibration or hydrogen bond coupled with COO was noted at $1661~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ for ASC, $1633~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for PSC and $1637~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ for standard [39]. The amide $\mathrm{II}$ of ASC, PSC and standard appeared at $1548~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , $1542~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and $1548~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , respectively. The Amide II peak arises due to $\mathrm{C-N}$ stretching and $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ in plane bending from amide linkages, including wagging vibrations of $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ groups from the glycine backbone and proline side-chains [40]. The amide III band was found at $1237~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for ASC, PSC and standard confirming presence of hydrogen bonds. Furthermore, the absorption peaks at around $1451{-}1452\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were also found in ASC and PSC, corresponded to vibration of pyrrolidine rings in proline and hydroxyproline [36].
## 3.6. CD and temperature induced changes in viscosity
CD spectrum is a distinctive method for protein secondary structure identification and studying thermal stability. CD spectra of isolated collagen over the temperature ranging from $25\mathrm{-}40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are shown in Fig. 3B. CD spectra showed rotatory maxima at $221~\mathrm{nm}$ , minima at 205 nm and a consistent crossover point (zero rotation) at around $212~\mathrm{nm}$ , which are characteristics of the triple helical conformation of this protein [41]. Fig. 3C represents the corresponding mean molar ellipticity, [θ] 221, as a function of temperature. The [θ] 221 values decreased with rise in temperature due to denaturation of the collagen triple helical structure. The denaturation temperature, $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ , was measured $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ where, starting and final temperatures of the denaturation were $32^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively.
The denaturation temperature $\mathrm{(T_{d})}$ was also determined by viscosity measurement of collagen in $0.1\textbf{M}$ acetic acid as depicted in Fig. 3D. The denaturation temperature $\mathrm{(T_{d})}$ via viscosity measurement was taken to be the temperature at which fractional viscosity was 0.5. $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of the extracted collagen was found to be $33~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is much closer to the $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of mammalian collagen. The $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of skin collagen of pig and calf (terrestrial mammals) is $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $40.8\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively
## 3.7. SEM observation of collagen sponge
Nano/micro fibrous architecture of collagen sponge having high porosity was prepared as shown in Fig. 4A. Interconnected porosity is an important parameter for cell adhesion, migration, and neo tissue formation. The porous structure further facilitates in absorbing wound fluid and supply oxygen to the wound. Thus the adequate pore size, morphology and fiber distribution in the collagen sponge were the advantageous factor that would promote cell adhesion and thereby tissue integration when implanted at the site of injury. The fibrillar morphology containing high surface area exposes small peptide cues like ‘RGD’ motifs present in the main collagen backbone and facilitates cell surface integrin-mediated adhesion [42].
## 3.8. Swelling and biodegradation of scaffolds
The water uptake capacity of a scaffold determines its potentiality to absorb moisture from exudating wound bed. It has been observed that a moist environment quickly initiates the epidermal cell migration and fasten the rate of wound re-epithelization twice compared to dry wounds [43]. Fig. 4D represents the swelling behavior of collagen scaffolds in PBS at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{~C~}$ .
The scaffolds exhibited rapid swelling of \~ $380\%$ within thirty minutes of incubation, which further increased to $\sim410~\%$ and achieved equilibrium. The high swelling behavior of the scaffold may be attributed to its high-interconnected porosity and hydrophilicity. Collagen sponges, having high water retaining capacity could act as a moist wound dressing, promoting rapid wound closure and faster healing.
Tissue regeneration and degradation kinetics of scaffold should be on par for proper wound healing. The collagen scaffold degraded gradually in lysozyme media at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as shown in Fig. 4E, and was complete by 18 days. Optimum swelling and degradation behavior of the collagen scaffold proposes itself as a promising material for wound healing application.
## 3.9. Cell growth on scaffold
Cytocompatibility of the scaffold is evaluated by cell growth, attachment, and proliferation studies. Fibroblast cells were seeded on the scaffold, and examined under SEM at different time interval as shown in Fig. 4 (B and C). After three days of incubation (Fig. 4B), cells attached and elongated with the development of lamelipodial protrusions. The growth and proliferation of fibroblasts enhanced by day 7 (Fig. 4C), where a much thicker and broader bundle of lamelipodial growth was seen indicating formation of cell sheath on the surface of the scaffold.
Cell growth kinetics was measured colorimetrically by evaluating its metabolic activity through MTT assay (Fig. 5A).  Quantitatively collagen scaffolds displayed $0.10{\pm}0.005$ , $0.37{\scriptstyle\pm0.01}$ , $0.54{\pm}0.02\$ and $0.06{\pm}0.03$ absorbance on day one, three, five, and seven, respectively, in comparison to $0.06{\scriptstyle\pm0.003}$ , 0.122±0.006, 0.186±0.009, and $0.226{\scriptstyle\pm0.01}$ in control, respectively. Tetrazolium dye is reduced to purple coloured formazan crystals, by cytosolic
NAD(P)H-dependent oxidoreductase enzymes largely in the cytosolic compartment of the cell. Therefore, a high absorbance value of collagen scaffold signifies presence of rapidly dividing cells.
## 3.10. Protein adsorption kinetics
Cell adhesion and protein adsorption, in particular, depends on the surface charge distribution, and net surface area of the scaffold. Therefore, protein adsorption studies give an insight about cell- material interaction. This method colorimetrically determines the total protein concentration by changing colour of the solution from green to purple in proportion to protein concentration (Fig. 5B). ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions of the copper (II) sulfate were reduced to $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ by the peptide bonds of the protein that had adhered to the scaffold. Thus, the amount of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ reduced is proportional to the amount of protein present in the solution.  Promotion of efficient and enhanced cell growth, a proportional increase in cell activity and protein adsorption on the scaffold entitles it to be used as material intended application.
## 3.11. Reverse transcriptase PCR of co-culture
Keratinocytes grown at air-liquid interface on the fibroblast-laden scaffolds stratified and expressed proteins related to it as shown in Fig. 5D. Keratin 14, a marker of dividing basal keratinocytes; keratin 10, marker of suprabasal, postmitotic cells and involucrin, a marker of terminal differentiation of epidermal keratinocytes, were expressed signifying formation of stratified epithelium [44]. Further E-Cadherine, whose role is eminent in in vitro calciuminduced intercellular junction organization and keratinocyte stratification was also expressed [45]. Collagen IV expression evidenced the formation of a basement membrane at dermoepidermal junction [46]. However, presence of laminin 5 was not detected, indicating more time needed for the development of a stable basement membrane. Collagen I, synthesized by fibroblast, is the major constituent of the extracellular matrix and helps in wound healing. As evident from the figure, collagen I, as expected, was abundantly expressed by the fibroblast cells. GAPDH served as the housekeeping gene.
## 3.12. TGF-β1 quantification by ELISA
TGF- $\upbeta1$ is an important regulatory factor which directly drives the deposition of ECM, fastening the wound healing process. However, prolonged expression of the protein in the later stages of the healing pathway leads to scarring [47]. Variations of TGF- $_{\cdot\beta1}$ expression of cocultured and mono-cultured scaffolds were shown in Fig 5C. TGF- $\upbeta1$ expression increased from third to the seventh day when scaffolds were grown with fibroblast cells. However, a reverse phenomenon was seen with decrease in TGF- $\upbeta1$ expression from third to the seventh day, when scaffolds were cultured with keratinocyte cells. Co- cultured samples too showed a decrease in TGF- $\upbeta1$ expression from third to seventh day. Therefore, it can be deduced that during the initial stages of healing when fibroblast is more active, the presence of collagen scaffold would fasten production of TGF- $\upbeta1$ , which in turn would recruit more fibroblast for ECM production. However, during later stages of healing presence of keratinocyte would suppress TGF- $_{\cdot\beta1}$ expression. Scarring, which is associated with high TGF- $_{\beta1}$ expression during the later stages of healing can thus be reduced.
## 3.13. Wound size measurements
The biological response of collagen sponge in wound healing on rat animal model was studied and compared with control. Postoperative effects such as pyogenesis, infection, and body fluid effusion were not evident in any animal throughout the experimental period. Wound healing progression (Fig. 6 (A-H)) and percentage of wound contraction in Fig. 6I, showed that wound area decreased in a time-dependent manner in both groups. On day 5, wound healing was similar in both the groups, but collagen transplanted wound displayed healing around the sharp corners of the wound in a regular circular appearance. However, the wound surface in the control group showed ulceration with fibrinous exudates. Later significant reduction in wound area was noted on day 10 $(80.51\%)$ and 15 $(98.97\%)$ in collagen-treated group in comparison to control group of day 10 $(60.95\%)$ and 15 $(85.87\%)$ . On day 10, healing in collagen-transplanted wound followed regulated, periphery to central region pattern where control group displayed irregular healing with island of skin formation, creating tension in surrounding tissue.  Hairs grew in the surrounding area of epithelialized wounds in both the groups. On day 15, collagen treated wound almost healed, having a newly grown epithelium layer with well-grown hairs, leaving the smaller area to be healed compared with the control group.
## 3.14. Histopathological finding
## 3.14.1. In vivo tissue-integration potential of collagen scaffold
The hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) and Masson's trichrome (MT) staining of collagen-treated wound area is shown in Fig. 7. On day 5, the scaffold structure started to disintegrate and was infiltrated by host cells, primarily neutrophils and lymphocytes; with the formation of new extracellular matrix (ECM), and neo-collagen by fibroblast (Fig. 7C). By day 10, the collagenous sponge continued to be filled with host cells, and ECM. Furthermore, the scaffold almost bioresorbed and degraded (Fig. 7H). By day 15, there was no observable scaffold remnant structure of the original matrix and the scaffold was completely bioresorbed while the space was filled with fibroblasts cells and newly formed collagen matrix (Fig. 7N).
3.14.2. Effect of collagen scaffolds on re-epithelialization, formation, and maturity of granulation tissue
The H&E and MT stain of wound areas of control, as well as collagen group, revealed the phenomenon of re-epithelization. At 5 day post-wounding, in control group advancing tongue of epidermal cells infiltrated into the wound bed under the scab (Fig. 7D). Similar results were evident in collagen-transplanted group (Fig. 7A). However, a significant difference was evidenced during formation of immature granulation tissue in control and collagen group; inflammatory cells and fibroblasts activity were more pronounced in collagen group as evidenced by Fig. 8 (A, B). Fibroblast cells were seen migrating from the dermal region towards the wound site for provisional matrix secretion and predominant collagen expression could be seen (Fig. 7C). In 10 days, healed regions of the collagen transplanted wound were covered by a stratified neoepithelium with significant fibroblast proliferation, granulation tissue formation and collagen deposition (Fig. 7I). However, the granulation tissue organization in unhealed regions of collagen-treated and control wound of day 10 was notably different. The collagen-treated group developed a well formed granulation tissue matrix beneath a thin epidermis (Fig. 7H) while in control group the granulation tissue had poorly developed beneath a thick layer of scab (Fig 7K). Significant difference in collagen distribution was also noted, with dark blue collagen staining in collagen-treated group (Fig. 7H) while light blue staining in control group (Fig. 7L). On day 15, the stratified epithelial layer thus formed in collagen group lay on a more mature dermal bed (Fig. 7M) while control group also showed well-formed stratified epithelial layer, granulation tissue formation and collagen deposition in healed regions (Fig. 7O), however, the unhealed regions still had ill-developed structures (Fig. 7P).
The H&E and MT staining sections, shown in Fig. 7, revealed that in collagen treated wound by day 10 (7I), skin appendages like hair follicles and sebaceous gland begins to appear, where the cells lining the hair follicle, helped in the formation of epidermal layer. On day 15, a sufficient number of fully formed hair follicles with sebaceous gland were present (7M).  In the control group, however, the presence of hair follicle and sebaceous gland were noted on day 15 (7O) yet much lesser in number as compared to collagen group.
## 3.15. Immunohistochemistry of the healed tissue
Immunohistochemical analysis of the reconstituted tissue in Fig. 9 shows positive staining for P63, a transcription factor, preferentially expressed in keratinocyte stem cells and essential for regenerative proliferation in epithelial development [48]. Further, the keratin 10, an epidermal marker of supra basal cell, was also well marked in the stratified epithelial layer formed after healing. Granulation tissue expresses $40\%$ type III collagen secreted by fibroblast cells [49]. Uniform staining throughout the newly formed matrix revealed presence of collagen III. The expression of these genes demonstrates stratified epithelialization along with the formation of a mature dermal tissue after healing by collagen sponge.
## Discussion
Collagen, the structural and functional component of dermal extracellular matrix, plays a vital role in wound healing process [49]. This fostered collagen as the most favoured material for dermal substitute. Collagen as healing matrix is used in different forms, namely nanofiber, sponge, and hydrogel. Development of nanofiber by electrospinning involves various critical criteria; along with the use of organic solvents that may rupture its native structure [50]. Contraction property of collagen hydrogel has been well reported limiting its use [51]. Current research on collagen based skin substitute mainly concentrates on biological or synthetic additives to the collagen to fasten wound healing. Here we document a collagen sponge as wound healing matrix from a potentially new source, mrigal fish scale, which is simpler to develop and works without any supportive material while healing acute full thickness wound faster than normal.
Mrigal has a significant production rate from aquaculture globally adding to huge accumulation of waste material. Thus, extraction of collagen not only enhances its economic value, but also beneficial for environment. In fish scale, collagen fibers are embedded in proteoglycan and randomly arranged along with hydroxyapatite mineral in external layer. While in the fibrillary layer, collagen fibers organize into lamellae, superimpose to produce an orthogonal and/or a double-twisted plywood pattern [28]. Fig. 1C shows the distribution and arrangement of collagen in the different layers of the mrigal scale. This collagen had the characteristic D-banding pattern similar to human dermal collagen, along with triple helical structure constituted of $({\tt a}_{1})_{2}{\tt a}_{2}$ . The extracted collagen was presumed to be collagen I; in accordance with reports suggesting collagen I as the predominant collagen in fish scale. Further, FTIR peaks and gel electrophoresis also proposed the extracted collagen to be collagen I. Imino acid content of collagen directly influences its thermal stability, while the imino acid content of collagen is influenced by habitat of an animal [52]. Degree of hydroxylation of proline residues determines stabilization of collagen triple helix. Degree of hydroxylation of proline was calculated to be $40\%$ , which is a factor enhancing the stability and the denaturation temperature of the extracted collagen. Additionally, the hydroxyl group of hydroxyproline and hydroxylysine by inter-chain hydrogen bonding affects thermal stability of the collagen molecule [53]. It has been reported that FWF has higher $\mathrm{T_{d,}}$ close to mammalian collagen owing to their habitat temperature [52]. Furthermore, an increase in imino acid content enhances the $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of collagen as well [34]. Additionally, the hydroxy group of hydroxyproline serine, threonine and hydroxylysine was found to get involved in intermolecular hydrogen bonding while pyrrolidine rings of imino acids stabilizes collagen by intramolecular hydrogen bonding [54]. Thus, comparable $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of mrigal scale collagen to mammalian collagen may promote its usability.
The extracted collagen could easily be lyophilized to develop into a highly interconnected porous sponge. Swelling behavior of the scaffold provided an insight about its high porosity and absorptivity which would be beneficial for absorbing exudates as well as keeping the wound moist. Ma et al. reported that increase in degree of cross-linking decreases swelling ratio of scaffold [6]. Following his observations, the collagen scaffolds were optimally cross-linked to maintain a high swelling ratio of $\sim410\%$ and slow degradation rate accounting to 18 days. Keratinocyte and fibroblast cells co-cultured on collagen sponge expressed basement membrane and ECM proteins as reported [46]. Further, stratified epidermal proteins also expressed highlighting the role of collagen sponge as a supporting material for skin cell growth, proliferation and differentiation.
Successful wound closer and repair relies on three phases of wound healing, namely inflammatory, proliferative and remodeling phase. The cascade and correlation of the three different phases depend on complex interactions between different cells including keratinocytes, fibroblasts, endothelial, immune cells, and other cell types. Keratinocytes adjacent to the wound site by altering their gene expression prepare themselves for migration over the wound bed [55]. During the progression of migrating epithelial tongue, keratinocytes start to proliferate and provide enough cells to cover the wound. As shown in Fig. 7C, the collagen sponge easily integrated with the wound bed ascribed to its cytocompatibility and excellent protein adsorption property. It fastened the epithelial tongue progression over the wound bed by providing a provisional matrix to migrate over; while the degradation products are chemotactic factors acting both on epithelial and dermal cells. Infiltration of fibroblast cells from the dermal region to the wound site increased profusely from $5^{\mathrm{th}}$ to $10^{\mathrm{th}}$ day in collagen-treated wound, secreting growth factors and extracellular matrix components, resulting in faster wound closer (Fig. 7H, I). The degradability of the collagen sponge complemented with the wound healing kinetics, where during early stages the scaffold had filled up the void spaces created by wound and later by degrading made the space available for new matrix deposition. Endothelial cells during the complex events of wound healing process contribute to two major roles: First, endothelium mediates, monitors leukocytes from intraluminal compartment to wound site, and, second, angiogenesis, and formation of new vessels during wound repair [56]. Both control and collagentreated groups had newly formed blood vessels from $\cdot$ day onwards (Fig. 7C, F). Epidermal stem cells (ESC), residing within specific microenvironments, govern the regenerative capacity of the skin. There are three distinct ESC niches reported: bulge of the hair follicle (HF), the base of the sebaceous gland, and the basal layer of the interfollicular epidermis (IFE). During epidermal injury, both the HF and IFE stem cells participate in re-epithelialization of the wound [57]. Formation of hair follicles and sebaceous gland in the healed region of collagen treated wound by $10^{\mathrm{th}}$ day signifies the above incidence. By $15^{\mathrm{th}}$ day even though control wound had unhealed de-epidermized region, collagen-treated group had completely healed with all dermal components, mature collagen matrix and stratified epidermis. The healing potential of the mrigal scale derived collagen sponge was better as compared to reports of collagen/silk or collagen/chitosan sponge [5, 6]. Both $\mathrm{~p~}63$ and keratin 10 presence is necessary for the formation of a stratified epithelial layer. Their expression proved that a well-constructed stratified epithelial layer with a mature dermal matrix, seen by collagen III expression, had formed by $15^{\mathrm{th}}$ day in collagen-treated group.
TGF- $\upbeta1$ has a paramount role in wound healing. Among various functions, its involvement in the expression of vital ECM proteins, such as fibronectin, collagen types I and III, and VEGF, and stimulating fibroblast contraction to enable wound closure is noteworthy. Further, integrins by binding to RGD motifs, which are well marked in the collagen sponge, activates the TGF- $\lvert\upbeta\rvert$ which in turn promotes keratinocyte migration. However, extended TGFβ1 expression can lead to scarring [47]. Results obtained by ELISA revealed that TGF- $\upbeta1$ levels synthesized predominantly by fibroblasts reduced over time in the presence of keratinocytes in skin substitute model, similar to the observations by Boyce et al [58]. In addition, it was found that monocultures of keratinocytes showed reduced expression of TGF- $\upbeta1$ . Therefore, Boyce et al. conclusively remarked that paracrine actions of keratinocytes prohibit TGF- $_{\cdot\beta1}$ expression by fibroblasts in healing skin. It can be presumed that down regulation of TGF- $\upbeta1$ after epithelization in in vitro model can mimic the in vivo situation after re-epithelization by the collagen sponge. Our hypothesis that collagen sponge might prevent scarring in vivo was also based on the report of Baxter et al. that reduction in TGF- $_{\cdot\beta1}$ level was noted in vivo on ${7^{\mathrm{th}}}$ day as compared to $3^{\mathrm{rd}}$ day, following chitosan sponge implantation [59]. However, in vivo studies need to be carried out for being conclusive about the anti- or reduced scarring property of collagen sponge.
## Conclusion
In summary, this study describes the isolation, characterization, fabrication and evaluation of a collagen sponge for its utility as dermal substitute. Type I was isolated from mrigal scale with yield of $\sim3\%$ (ASC) and \~ $7\%$ (PSC) and D- spacing was calculated to be \~ 65 nm. It had the characteristic $({\tt a}_{1})_{2}{\tt a}_{2}$ triple helical constitution with major FTIR peaks. Thermal denaturation temperature $(\mathrm{T_{d}}\sim35^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ was significantly higher than many fresh water fishes reported elsewhere, owing to its high imino acid content. Collagen sponges were prepared by freeze–drying a homogeneous aqueous solution. Collagen scaffolds were microfibrous and highly porous with a swelling ratio of \~ $410\%$ and slower degradation rate of 18 days. Cytocompatibility and protein adsorption capability of the scaffold was appreciable. Reconstituted stratified epidermal layers with basement membrane proteins and extracellular matrix components were evidenced in in vitro skin equivalent model developed by co-culturing fibroblast and keratinocyte cells. Reduction in TGF- $\upbeta1$ level was also noted, implying its antiscarring property. The collagen sponge increased the wound healing rate, re-epithelialization, and dermal reconstitution in vivo. The results presented in this study suggest collagen sponge from mrigal fish scale as a promising dermal substitute.
